WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:05.280
The recent arrival of the long-awaited&nbsp;
"Spider-Man: No Way Home" was thrilling for many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.560
reasons, but the promise of seeing more than one&nbsp;
of Spidey's biggest foes join the Marvel Cinematic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:16.240
Universe for the first time arguably had fans&nbsp;
more excited than any other aspect of its release.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.160
Ever since Alfred Molina revealed that&nbsp;
he was reprising the role of Doc Ock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.640
a character he first portrayed in&nbsp;
Sam Raimi's "Spider-Man 2" in 2004,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:28.720
franchise devotees began to eagerly&nbsp;
anticipate the chance to see he and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:33.520
various villains from the other "Spider-Man"&nbsp;
films take on Tom Holland's Peter Parker.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:37.760
Jaime Foxx's Electro is one of the&nbsp;
antagonists from a prior "Spider-Man" movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:41.200
who made their MCU debut in "No Way Home." Electro&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.680
first appeared on the big screen in&nbsp;
2014's "The Amazing Spider-Man 2."

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:50.560
Though the film is considered one of the worst to&nbsp;
star the famed web-slinger, Electro was certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:55.680
a memorable villain, making his reintroduction in&nbsp;
the latest "Spider-Man" film a welcome inclusion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.360
in spite of the poor reception of&nbsp;
his first film appearance. That said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:03.840
the actor recently revealed that he initially&nbsp;
felt hesitant to reprise the role prior to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.640
accepting — and would only return if one&nbsp;
condition in particular was met first.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:14.480
MCU architect Kevin Feige and Sony producer&nbsp;
Amy Pascal recently sat down with Brandon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:20.240
Davis from Comicbook.com, and amidst a discussion&nbsp;
of various other topics related to "Spider-Man:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.000
No Way Home," the trio eventually&nbsp;
got to talking about Electro.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.240
When the character first appeared in "The&nbsp;
Amazing Spider-Man 2," many fans were upset&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:33.200
by the character's full body-spanning electric&nbsp;
blue color, which was a far cry from his look in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:38.560
the comics. However, trailers for "Spider-Man:&nbsp;
No Way Home" revealed that in his latest film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:42.800
Electro would be outfitted in a costume much&nbsp;
more akin to how the character appeared in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:48.000
his comic book source material — a costume&nbsp;
which also features an Iron Man arc reactor.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:53.040
This subject came up when Davis asked Feige if&nbsp;
Electro lost his blue appearance because of that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:58.400
arc reactor. Feige, a veteran when it comes&nbsp;
to avoiding spoiling his own movies, replied,

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.760
"I won't say that you're wrong. I&nbsp;
also won't say that you're right."

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:06.080
He did share, however, that the studios promised&nbsp;
Foxx that Electro wouldn't be blue this time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:10.960
around. Foxx himself has publicly shared his&nbsp;
distaste for the blue costume. Speaking at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:16.800
the CCXP panel, he expressed his enthusiasm&nbsp;
for the new take on the character in the MCU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.360
saying he was "excited" for the change.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:22.480
Electro is one of the more&nbsp;
sympathetic "Spider-Man" villains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:25.840
and the comic-accurate revision&nbsp;
to his appearance in "No Way Home"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.920
indicates Marvel's commitment to bringing&nbsp;
this character to life the right way.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:33.440
Following the release of "Spider-Man:&nbsp;
No Way Home" on December 17,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:37.680
Jamie Foxx further discussed returning to&nbsp;
the role of Electro in an interview published&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:42.080
on the official Marvel website. After Foxx&nbsp;
reiterated that the promise of the character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:46.560
sporting a new appearance contributed to him&nbsp;
accepting the role, he went on to explain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:50.480
how Electro's acknowledgement of his new&nbsp;
look in the movie's script was integral to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:56.000
pulling off such a drastic alteration to his&nbsp;
previously established character. Foxx said,

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.120
"The [biggest] thing that changed is&nbsp;
that he acknowledged the way he looked.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:04.240
He acknowledged, 'Oh, I like this, I dig this.'&nbsp;
By doing that, it answers any questions like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:08.320
why or what's this thing? Being able to do&nbsp;
that, and just bringing a little of the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.720
I wanted to talk and how I wanted to present&nbsp;
myself, [Electro] has a little hipness to him."

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:16.800
Foxx had further kind things to say about&nbsp;
his character's new look in response to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:21.760
a question about the fact that his updated&nbsp;
costume resembles an electrician's uniform:

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:26.720
"It's electrician-chic. What I'm saying is that&nbsp;
it's fly. It's not a cape or anything like that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.000
I really dug that. That's cool&nbsp;
because it keeps it practical.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.680
'Practical magic' is what you could&nbsp;
call it, because he's so practical."

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:37.680
So, while he appears to have disliked the look&nbsp;
of the character the first time around, Foxx&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:41.840
offered nothing but praise for both the process&nbsp;
through which the character was redesigned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:45.840
and the appearance they ultimately landed&nbsp;
on during the production of "No Way Home."

